.TH MADC 1 "2026-07-23" "madc 0.38.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
madc \- My Advanced Dialect of C: C/C++ JIT compiler, native toolchain, and script runner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B madc
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIsource\fR [\fIprogram-args\fR...]
.br
.B madc
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIcompile_commands.json\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B madc
\fB\-o\fR \fIprog\fR | \fB\-c\fR | \fB\-shared\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIsource\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B madc
parses a C/C++ dialect into an intermediate C11 tree, lowers it through
c2mir to MIR, and JIT-executes it \(em or compiles it ahead of time to a
native ELF executable, relocatable object, or shared library with no
external assembler or linker. It can also emit portable C11 source.
.PP
Programs run like scripts: a file of top-level statements needs no
.BR main ()
(one is synthesized), a
.B #!/usr/bin/env madc
shebang works, and common library identifiers such as
.BR printf ()
resolve without any #include via auto-header inclusion. Standard C and
POSIX headers are embedded in the binary. Helper namespaces
(php::, perl::, python::, ruby::, js::, rust::) bring familiar functions
from other languages into native C code.
.PP
Arguments after the source file are passed to the program as
.IR argc / argv .
.SH INPUT MODES
.TP
.I file
Compile and JIT-run a single source file.
.TP
.BI \-\-project " db.json"
Build from a
.IR compile_commands.json :
compile each translation unit, link the modules in-process, and run the
entry point. A bare
.I .json
argument implies \fB\-\-project\fR.
.TP
.I file.o
Execute a madc-compiled relocatable object (\fB\-c\fR output) as a
precompiled cache \(em no recompilation.
.TP
.B \-E
Preprocess only (print the expanded source).
.SH LANGUAGE / PREPROCESSOR OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-std= std
Language standard:
.BR c89 / c90 / c99 / c11 / c17 / c23
(\fBc\fR = c11), \fBc++\fR\fINN\fR, or \fBmadc\fR (the default dialect;
C++ keywords reserved). A
.I .c
translation unit under \fB\-\-project\fR defaults to C mode.
.TP
.BI \-stdlib= lib
C++ standard library flavor, in clang's spelling:
.B libstdc++
or
.BR libc++ ,
whichever this build could probe \(em an unknown or unbuilt flavor is an
error naming the ones this binary actually has. Selecting a flavor
.I replaces
the C++ include search list, exactly as clang's driver does; it is not
the same as putting the library first with
.BR \-I ,
because a library reaches the C headers behind it through
.BR #include_next ,
which would otherwise walk into the other library's directories.
Orthogonal to the target: a Mach\-O target
.I defaults
to libc++, it never
.I means
libc++.
.TP
.BI \-D name\fR[=\fIvalue\fR]
Define a preprocessor macro.
.TP
.BI \-I dir
Add an include search directory.
.TP
.BI \-l name
dlopen
.IB lib name .so
(RTLD_GLOBAL) so its symbols resolve at link time (e.g. \fB\-lcrypt\fR).
In AOT mode this becomes a DT_NEEDED entry instead.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-load
Do not act on #load directives; link explicitly via \fB\-l\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-includes
Do not process #include directives.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-embedded\-headers
Disable the baked-in headers; use real system headers.
.SH CODEGEN OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-O0 " .. " \-O3
JIT optimization level (bare \fB\-O\fR = \fB\-O1\fR).
.TP
.B \-g
Debug info: gdb can break, step, and inspect the JIT'd or AOT-compiled
program (forces \fB\-O0\fR).
.TP
.BI \-fno\-builtin\- name
Disable a specific builtin.
.TP
.B \-\-finstrument\-functions
Emit __cyg_profile instrumentation hooks.
.SH AOT OUTPUT (do not run)
.TP
.BR \-c " [" \-o " \fIfile.o\fR]"
Compile to a relocatable native object (default:
.IR source-base .o).
.TP
.BI \-o " prog"
Compile to a native executable \(em MIR assembles the ELF directly.
The produced program depends on
.B libmadc.so.0
only when it uses the madc runtime; a plain C program's executable is
runtime-free.
.TP
.BR \-shared " [" \-o " \fIfile.so\fR]"
Compile to a shared object (ET_DYN; dlopen/#load-consumable; PIC).
.TP
.BR \-static\-libmadc ", " \-static
Carry the madc runtime pieces the program needs INSIDE the emitted
image, so it runs with no madc library installed at all
.RB ( gcc "'s " \-static\-libgcc " precedent)."
The pieces come from this madc's AOT ledger \(em the C\-lane runtime
compiled to MIR modules and carried in its frozen\-forest container, so
a packed or installed madc has one and a bare development build does
not (point at a container with
.BR \-\-forest\-bind= \fIfile\fR).
Covers the C lane: try/catch/throw and VLA scope exit. A program that
needs the C++ script\-lane runtime refuses at emit, naming the symbols.
libc and libstdc++ stay dynamic \(em the flag scopes exactly what it
promises;
.B \-static
is an alias (full\-static cannot exist on darwin, which has no static
libSystem). Requires a linked output: the runtime merges at the link
that produces the final image, not into a
.BR \-c " .o."
Note that a
.B \-static\-libmadc
image carries PROCESS\-GLOBAL exception state rather than per\-thread:
MIR has no thread\-local storage.
.SH SOURCE OUTPUT (do not run)
.TP
.B \-\-emit=c11\fR|\fBmc11
Render the program as portable C11 (or MC11) source on stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-pch
Write a .madh precompiled header.
.TP
.BI \-\-emit\-function " name"
Print one function's source.
.TP
.B \-dM
Preprocess, then print the effective macro table (gcc \fB\-dM \-E\fR
analogue).
.SH MISC OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Print input/token/timing stats to stderr after the run.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose / debug output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-? ", " \-\-help
Show the full option list, including the frozen-forest (compile-once)
and dump/diagnostic options not listed here.
.TP
.BI \-\-config= file
Read this configuration file instead of searching for one (see
.B FILES
below). A named file that cannot be loaded is an error \(em never
silently ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Skip the configuration-file search entirely, so no ambient file can
affect the compile. Build systems and test harnesses should pass this.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BI MADC_CPU_LIMIT= secs
Arm an RLIMIT_CPU guard. Default: off \(em madc also runs the program,
so no finite default is safe. Intended for embedding hosts and
sandboxes.
.TP
.BI MADC_MEM_LIMIT= MB
Address-space guard (RLIMIT_AS, covers JIT mappings). Default: 4096 MB
plus 128 MB per \fB\-\-project\fR translation unit; 0 disables. A
tripped guard names the knob in its error message.
.TP
.BI MADC_FOREST= file
Frozen forest container to bind when no earlier discovery arm carries
one. The chain, first usable container wins: the binary's own image,
the
.B libmadc
image when madc is linked shared, the \fI<exe>\fB.forest\fR and
\fI<lib>\fB.forest\fR sidecars, this path, and finally the
.B forest
key of a configuration file.
.TP
.BI XDG_CONFIG_HOME= dir
Where the configuration-file search looks for the per-user file
(\fIdir\fB/madc/madc.ini\fR); defaults to \fB~/.config\fR.
.SH FILES
.B madc
optionally reads one
.B madc.ini
configuration file. Settings resolve in this order, most specific first:
.B command line
>
.B environment
>
.B madc.ini
> built-in defaults.
.PP
Unless
.BI \-\-config= file
or
.B \-\-no\-config
is given, these are tried in order and the
.I first existing
file wins outright \(em configuration files are never merged:
.PP
.nf
    ./madc.ini
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/madc/madc.ini   (or ~/.config/madc/madc.ini)
    <sysconfdir>/madc.ini            (baked at build time)
.fi
.PP
The format is \fBkey = value\fR, one per line, with
.B #
or
.B ;
comments and an optional
.B [madc]
section header. Values may be quoted. A relative path resolves against
the configuration file's own directory, and a leading
.B ~/
expands. An unknown key, a malformed line, or a non-numeric limit is an
.I error
\(em a configuration file that half-applies is worse than one that
refuses. Keys:
.TP
.BI std= dialect
Default language standard, as for
.BR \-\-std= .
A
.B \-\-std=
on the command line overrides it.
.TP
.BI stdlib= lib
Default C++ standard library flavor, as for
.BR \-stdlib= .
A
.B \-stdlib=
on the command line overrides it.
.TP
.BI forest= file
Frozen forest container, used as the last discovery arm (see
.B MADC_FOREST
above, which outranks it).
.TP
.BI include= dir
Additional include directory, repeatable, searched after every
.B \-I
directory from the command line. It is applied through the same path as
.BR \-I ,
so it behaves identically \(em including in
.B \-\-project
mode, where the manifest's own include paths are what each translation
unit gets.
.TP
.BI cpu\-limit= secs
Default for
.BR MADC_CPU_LIMIT ;
.B 0
disables. The environment variable overrides it.
.TP
.BI mem\-limit= MB
Default for
.BR MADC_MEM_LIMIT ;
.B 0
disables. The environment variable overrides it.
.PP
Configuration-file support is a build-time option
(\fBconfigure \-\-disable\-config\-file\fR removes it, which embedding
hosts and sandboxed deployments want: a file that can redirect where the
compiler loads frozen state from is an attack surface).
.B libmadc
itself never reads a configuration file \(em this is a
.BR madc (1)
feature, and an embedding host configures its compiles through the
library API.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a file as a script (no main, no includes needed):
.PP
.nf
    $ echo 'printf("%d\\n", 6 * 7);' > answer.mad
    $ madc answer.mad
    42
.fi
.PP
Run a C program with arguments:
.PP
.nf
    $ madc \-\-std=c17 prog.c arg1 arg2
.fi
.PP
Build and run a multi-file project, then ship it as one native binary:
.PP
.nf
    $ madc \-\-project compile_commands.json
    $ madc \-\-project compile_commands.json \-o app
.fi
.PP
Transpile to portable C11:
.PP
.nf
    $ madc \-\-emit=c11 prog.mad > prog.c
.fi
